            "target_entity_type": "NaturalHazardType",
            "paragraph": "Drought is a temporary and relative condition characterized by a deficit in available water within a specific period and region (Wilhite 2000). According to Wilhite and Glantz (1985) and American Meteorological Society (2004) drought can be categorized into four main types: meteorological, agricultural, hydrological and socio-economic droughts. All these drought categories are mainly linked to rainfall regime variations, which effects reflected respectively on soil moisture conditions, productive yield of crops, streamflow regime and the failure of water resources to meet water demand. Despite hydrological drought may resulted in a reduction of water resources for both surface and groundwater, Mishra and Singh (2010) introduced for the first time the concept of groundwater drought and how this can be treated as a new type of drought. Specifically, the study assessed that when a groundwater drought occurs primarily groundwater recharge and later groundwater levels as groundwater discharge decrease, considering a time scale of months to years (Van Lanen and Peters 2000). Consequently, the effects of a drought period impact groundwater slower than surface water, exhibiting initially buffer impacts respect to surface processes (Cuthbert et al. 2023; Hellwig et al. 2020).",
